    D3: the target beta/sqrt(Z~) = 1/sqrt(8) = 0.353553 is hit EXACTLY by 9 members of the set:
        F1 sqrt(T(F)/T(adj)) SO(10)                          = 0.353553
        F1 sqrt(T(F)/T(adj)) SU(4)                           = 0.353553
        F1 sqrt(T(F)/T(adj)) Sp(6)                           = 0.353553
        F2 sqrt(k/(k+h)) SO(16) k=2                          = 0.353553
        F2 sqrt(k/(k+h)) SO(9) k=1                           = 0.353553
        F2 sqrt(k/(k+h)) SU(7) k=1                           = 0.353553
        F2 sqrt(k/(k+h)) Sp(12) k=1                          = 0.353553
        F5 1/sqrt(m) m=8                                     = 0.353553
        F6 sqrt(rank/dim) SU(7)                              = 0.353553
    D3: these are STRUCTURALLY DIFFERENT constructions -- an index ratio, a current-algebra level, and a bare index -- that
        happen to coincide because lambda = 8 is a SMALL INTEGER and small integers are what all of them produce.  8 is also
        dim(adjoint SU(3)), h^v(SU(8)), and 2^3.  Reporting them is the point; adopting any of them would be the error this
        lane exists to prevent.  Nothing selects SU(4) over SU(5), or level 1 over level 2, except the answer.
  [FAIL] D3 [S10, uniqueness] the exact hit on 1/sqrt(8) is reached by ONE construction, so the group and representation are singled out   (9 structurally different group-theoretic constructions land EXACTLY on 1/sqrt(8): F1 sqrt(T(F)/T(adj)) SO(10); F1 sqrt(T(F)/T(adj)) SU(4); F1 sqrt(T(F)/T(adj)) Sp(6); F2 sqrt(k/(k+h)) SO(16) k=2; F2 sqrt(k/(k+h)) SO(9) k=1; F2 sqrt(k/(k+h)) SU(7) k=1; F2 sqrt(k/(k+h)) Sp(12) k=1; F5 1/sqrt(m) m=8; F6 sqrt(rank/dim) SU(7).  Each would read as a derivation of kappa = 1/2 if quoted alone.  That they disagree about WHICH group and WHICH level is the proof that none of them derives anything -- and it is precisely the failure mode this repository has been burned by before)
    D4: free choices in a coset construction: 5 -- the group G; the subgroup H / the embedding; the representation R of the order parameter; the level k (or the index), where the construction has one; which invariant carries the stiffness vs the coupling
        With the isotropy representation IRREDUCIBLE the last choice disappears and the answer is forced to kappa = sqrt(2) (D1,
        refuted).  With it REDUCIBLE, the number of independent invariant metrics equals the number of irreducible summands,
        each with its own free normalisation -- so the coefficient becomes a CONTINUOUS ratio again, not a group-theoretic one.
  [FAIL] D4 [S10, freedom] the coset route has zero free choices once the physical requirement (an order parameter with rho_vac ~ X^2, a0 ~ X) is imposed   (5 free choices, and they form a dichotomy with no interior: IRREDUCIBLE isotropy forces kappa = sqrt(2) (21 sigma out, D1), REDUCIBLE isotropy restores one free normalisation per summand and the coefficient is continuous again.  There is no coset that is rigid AND lands in the band)

    S10 VERDICT: RELOCATES.  Free choices = 5.  Candidate set 557 with 97 in band vs the 27-member numerology baseline;
                 9 exact hits on 1/sqrt(8) from different groups.  Its zero-choice form is REFUTED at kappa = sqrt(2) (D1).  L32's hope that a coset
                 could supply N4 is NOT borne out: the rigidity it has is rigidity about the WRONG number, and the freedom it needs to reach 0.354 destroys the rigidity.

