  OUTCOME: none of L32's three survivors derives kappa, and the way each fails is now specific.
           S8 HORIZON THERMODYNAMICS is an IDENTIFICATION, and the question is settled: the 2 pi in the Unruh formula and the 2 pi in
           the Gibbons-Hawking formula are the SAME 2 pi and CANCEL when the temperatures are equated, so the only identity-forced
           statement is a0 = c H_Lambda (kappa = 2.894, 56 sigma out).  Reaching 0.4607 requires putting the 2 pi back in by
           hand, and a change of H0 convention converts the result into kappa = 1/2 to 1% in a0.
           S9 TRANSMUTATION earns its LOCK honestly -- the coupling, the matching scale and the beta-function coefficient all cancel --
           and then fails rigidity completely: every discrete datum of the sector cancels WITH them, and what survives is a
           non-perturbative prefactor that moves from inside the band to outside it with the renormalisation scheme alone.
           S10 COSET, which L32 flagged as the only structure that could supply RIGIDITY, turns out to be rigid about the wrong number:
           a single invariant metric forces kappa = sqrt(2) (21 sigma out), and every construction that can reach 0.354 needs the group,
           the embedding, the representation and the level chosen.  Its candidate set puts 97 members in the band against the
           27-member numerology baseline, and hits 1/sqrt(8) EXACTLY 9 times from different groups.
           L3's a0-DEPENDENT MEMBRANE is closed here by a new theorem (T7): a beta-dependent charge or tension is a pincer -- rigidity
           in beta forces either a0 ~ Lambda^1 (LOCK broken) or kappa ~ T_0/e_0 (continuous).
           And a bound that outlives all four: the 3-sigma band maps to lambda = Z~/beta^2 in [4.6, 12.4], containing 8 integers,
           so even a PERFECT integer derivation of lambda = 8 would be worth 2.0 bits = 1.7 sigma-equivalent -- and lambda = 8 is a
           CANONICAL-FOOTING statement (5.51 on the alt footing, not an integer at all).
           kappa remains FITTED.  Nothing here derives it, and nothing here makes 1/2 preferred over 0.461.

